1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,943
Lesson 7.2.

2
00:00:02,323 --> 00:00:03,566
Shor's algorithm.

3
00:00:04,683 --> 00:00:09,156
In nineteen ninety-four, Peter
Shor showed that a quantum computer

4
00:00:09,156 --> 00:00:13,018
finds the order in time
polynomial in the number of bits.

5
00:00:13,398 --> 00:00:16,388
It is the benchmark against
which we will compare diffusion.

6
00:00:16,768 --> 00:00:21,048
Today we walk through its four
steps with a small example, N =

7
00:00:21,048 --> 00:00:25,052
21, which we can simulate
exactly on a classical computer.

8
00:00:26,183 --> 00:00:32,279
First, a uniform superposition of
all exponents x from zero to Q − 1 is

9
00:00:32,279 --> 00:00:37,516
prepared, with Q a power of two
larger than N²; here Q = 512.

10
00:00:37,896 --> 00:00:43,873
Second, a reversible circuit computes
a^x mod N in a second register.

11
00:00:44,253 --> 00:00:49,492
With a = 2, the values repeat every
six exponents: the order is six.

12
00:00:49,872 --> 00:00:53,162
When the second register is
measured, the first is left in a

13
00:00:53,162 --> 00:00:56,397
superposition of exponents
separated exactly by the period.

14
00:00:57,517 --> 00:01:02,486
Third, the quantum Fourier transform
turns that periodicity into peaks.

15
00:01:02,866 --> 00:01:06,696
The amplitudes of outcomes that
do not fit the period cancel

16
00:01:06,696 --> 00:01:09,953
by interference, and those
that fit are reinforced.

17
00:01:10,333 --> 00:01:14,880
In the example, the probability
concentrates near the

18
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:19,599
multiples of Q/r, about 85.3:
at zero, eighty-five, one

19
00:01:19,599 --> 00:01:24,233
hundred seventy-one, two
hundred fifty-six, and so on.

20
00:01:25,350 --> 00:01:27,822
Fourth, classical post-processing.

21
00:01:28,202 --> 00:01:32,263
An outcome y satisfies y/Q ≈ k/r.

22
00:01:32,643 --> 00:01:35,461
The continued-fraction
algorithm finds the best

23
00:01:35,461 --> 00:01:37,920
fraction with denominator smaller than N.

24
00:01:38,300 --> 00:01:45,516
For y = 85, 85/512 is approximated
by one sixth: the order is six.

25
00:01:45,896 --> 00:01:51,574
But for y = 171 we get one third, and
only obtain a divisor of the order.

26
00:01:51,954 --> 00:01:55,476
In this example, a single
run gives the order directly

27
00:01:55,476 --> 00:01:59,193
with probability thirty-one
percent; combining a few runs

28
00:01:59,193 --> 00:02:02,454
with the least common
multiple always recovers it.

29
00:02:03,583 --> 00:02:04,474
How much does it cost?

30
00:02:04,854 --> 00:02:09,289
Modular exponentiation dominates:
with schoolbook arithmetic it is

31
00:02:09,289 --> 00:02:13,119
on the order of n³ gates, with
n the number of bits of N.

32
00:02:13,499 --> 00:02:16,331
The Fourier transform costs n².

33
00:02:16,711 --> 00:02:18,804
Everything is polynomial, against the

34
00:02:18,804 --> 00:02:21,801
subexponential cost of the
best classical algorithms.

35
00:02:22,181 --> 00:02:24,723
The price is in the hardware: thousands of

36
00:02:24,723 --> 00:02:28,595
error-corrected logical qubits,
which do not yet exist at scale.

37
00:02:29,717 --> 00:02:32,465
Let's keep the central idea
for the rest of the unit.

38
00:02:32,845 --> 00:02:35,855
Shor amplifies Fourier
peaks by interference:

39
00:02:35,855 --> 00:02:38,465
frequencies that do not fit cancel out.

40
00:02:38,845 --> 00:02:44,425
Diffusion will do the opposite: it will
damp all nonzero Fourier modes, and what

41
00:02:44,425 --> 00:02:49,169
survives, the uniform distribution
over the group, reveals its size.

42
00:02:49,549 --> 00:02:52,688
Interference that amplifies
versus dissipation that erases.

43
00:02:53,817 --> 00:02:58,643
Shor threatens public-key cryptography
and motivates post-quantum standards.

44
00:02:59,023 --> 00:03:02,983
The same period-finding core
solves discrete logarithms.

45
00:03:03,363 --> 00:03:08,978
Experimental demonstrations so far factor
tiny numbers with simplified circuits.

46
00:03:09,358 --> 00:03:13,638
In lab ten you will simulate the
distribution and the continued fractions.

47
00:03:14,750 --> 00:03:18,201
Common mistakes: believing
the quantum computer

48
00:03:18,201 --> 00:03:21,285
tries all exponents and reads all answers.

49
00:03:21,665 --> 00:03:24,432
Taking the denominator as the
order without verifying it.

50
00:03:24,812 --> 00:03:27,672
Forgetting that some
outcomes give only a divisor.

51
00:03:28,052 --> 00:03:30,808
And confusing logical
qubits with physical qubits.

52
00:03:31,917 --> 00:03:35,678
In summary: Shor prepares a
superposition, computes modular

53
00:03:35,678 --> 00:03:40,078
exponentiation, applies the Fourier
transform to turn the period into

54
00:03:40,078 --> 00:03:44,350
probability peaks, and recovers
the order with continued fractions.

55
00:03:44,730 --> 00:03:47,396
Its cost is polynomial
in the number of bits.

56
00:03:47,776 --> 00:03:51,491
It interferes to amplify;
diffusion will dissipate to erase.

57
00:03:52,617 --> 00:03:57,074
To practice: explain why
Q must be at least N².

58
00:03:57,454 --> 00:03:59,980
Expand the continued
fraction of the example.

59
00:04:00,360 --> 00:04:02,683
Combine two runs that give divisors.

60
00:04:03,063 --> 00:04:04,973
And say which stage dominates the cost.
